From 0eaff2a78e1605f86881196890c76cdb857e1490 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tillmann Severin <tillmann.severin@mailbox.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:42:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm: hisilicon: add a driver for the STB display processor

The video display processor found on HiSilicon set-top-box SoCs composes
several video and graphics layers into one or more display channels.  Add
a driver that takes over the first graphics layer of the first display
channel, which is the one the bootloader leaves scanning out.

The display timing is not programmed.  Both the channel and the HDMI
transmitter are already running by the time Linux starts, and neither the
pixel clock nor the transmitter is described yet, so the mode is read back
out of the timing registers and offered as the only mode of a fixed
connector.  Everything the bootloader put into the layer control register
beyond the pixel format is preserved, because the blending and bus request
settings it holds are not otherwise known.

That is enough for a framebuffer console and for page flipping against a
real vertical blank, which the display channel reports through its first
vertical timing threshold.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
